<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arianism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–7th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arianism was a Christian doctrine, named after the Alexandrian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, holding that the Son was created by the Father and not of the same substance. Condemned at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325, it spread among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Rugians and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, setting their rulers apart from the Nicene Roman population. It faded after the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 589.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arianism.jpg" alt="Arianism" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ввласенко · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baptism_of_Christ._Mosaic_in_Arian_Baptistry._Ravenna,_Italy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Arianism is traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, a presbyter who preached and studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, though modern scholars treat the fourth-century controversy as the result of deeper tensions already present among Christians and describe Arius as a theological conservative. They note that no single Arian party existed; those who opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a> formed a loose coalition united by opposition to the term homoousios. The name Arian was a polemical term used by outsiders, not what the followers called themselves. In 321 a synod at Alexandria denounced Arius for teaching a heterodox view of the relation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> to God the Father, and his followers' influence in the schools of Alexandria spread his views, especially in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Little of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>'s own work survives except in quotations chosen by his opponents. His basic premise was that only God is independent of existing, so the Son, being dependent, must be called a creature. In his words, if the Father begat the Son, the one begotten had a beginning, and there was a time when the Son was not. The Logos was a divine being created by the Father before the world, serving as the medium of creation and subordinate to the Father.</p><p>In 325 the Emperor Constantine called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a>, which condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a> and formulated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, whose central term homoousios means of the same substance. Arius, not being a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, could not sit, and Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> spoke for his position. After Constantine's death in 337 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> encouraged the Arians and exiled Nicene bishops, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius of Alexandria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Arianism was effectively wiped out among the ruling class of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Eastern Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, who held the Nicene faith. The missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>, an Arian bishop, translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into Gothic and converted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and Arianism spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Langobards</a>, Svevi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>. In the Germanic kingdoms of the 5th century separate Arian and Nicene churches existed side by side, the elites Arian and the Romance-majority population Nicene.</p><p>Organised Arianism survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> until suppressed in the 6th and 7th centuries. Visigothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reccared-i">Reccared I</a> at the Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 589, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Grimoald and his son Garibald, who reigned from 662 to 671, were the last Arian kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arianism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then defeated the Alemanni and, at a date disputed between about 496 and 508, was baptised a Catholic Christian at Reims by Bishop Remigius, rather than adopting the <b>Arian Christianity</b> of the other Germanic kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Little Visigothic settlement occurred elsewhere; the Goths were always a minority among the Hispano-Romans, and by the 7th century the two had become inextricably intertwined, the Gothic language losing its last function as a church language when <b>Arianism</b> was renounced in 589.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Spain it lost its last, probably already declining, role as a church language when the Visigoths converted from <b>Arianism</b> to Nicene Christianity in 589, though it survived as a language of the home in the Iberian Peninsula as late as the eighth century.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It had condemned <b>Arianism</b>, adopted the Nicene Creed and set rules for dating Easter, shaping Christian doctrine for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It condemned <b>Arianism</b> and produced the first version of the Nicene Creed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-20">20 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings were <b>Arians</b>, but Sigismund converted to Catholic Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-burgundians">Kingdom of the Burgundians</a></li></ul></section>
